  "explanation": {
    "what_it_is": "Ginseng is a root from plants in the Panax genus, traditionally used in Asian medicine for over 2,000 years. It contains active compounds called ginsenosides, which are thought to drive most of its biological effects.",
    "how_it_works": "Ginsenosides appear to work through multiple pathways: they modulate the nervous system's stress response (via the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis), improve blood vessel function and blood flow, dampen excessive immune inflammatory signals, and influence neurotransmitter activity related to mood and cognition. These actions affect energy production, vascular health, and how the body handles physical and mental stressors.",
    "proven_benefits": [
      {
        "benefit": "Cardiovascular health",
        "grade": "Strong",
        "note": "Human evidence from 20+ studies supports benefits for blood pressure, blood vessel function, and overall cardiovascular outcomes."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Inflammation reduction",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "Moderate human evidence (30 studies) indicates ginseng can lower inflammatory markers, though effects are modest in many trials."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Stress and anxiety resilience",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "Moderate human evidence (34 studies) shows ginseng may reduce perceived stress and anxiety symptoms, likely through nervous system modulation."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Mood and depression",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "Moderate human evidence (25 studies) suggests ginseng may improve mood and depressive symptoms, particularly when combined with other treatments."
      },
      {
        "benefit": "Immune function",
        "grade": "Moderate",
        "note": "Moderate human evidence (30 studies) indicates ginseng may enhance certain immune markers and reduce infection risk, though effects vary by type and dose."
      }
    ],
    "promising_benefits": [],
    "emerging_research": "Recent work explores ginseng's potential role in cognitive aging, athletic endurance, and blood sugar regulation, but these findings remain preliminary and mostly limited to smaller studies or animal models. More large, well-controlled human trials are needed to clarify benefits in these areas.",
    "caveats": "Quality and ginsenoside content vary widely between commercial products; standardized extracts (4\u20137% ginsenosides) are preferred for consistency. Ginseng may interact with blood thinners, stimulants, and diabetes medications, and some people report sleep disruption if taken late in the day.",
